WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:30.332
ai can now create art by painting with hundreds of thousands of virtual brushstrokes instead of pixels. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 229. A deep dive on stroke order, first jobs in Australia, and QuickBits. And Thropic, an ai company the decoder covered, has a trained system or model called Claude Fable 5.2. It writes Python so a virtual canvas gets painted stroke by stroke. Python is a language for listing instructions a computer can

00:00:30.332 --> 00:00:36.752
follow, one after another. Ordinary image tools pick every tiny screen dot's color in a single pass,

00:00:37.232 --> 00:00:43.113
like a photo appearing fully formed. Each stroke here carries bristle texture and notes on how

00:00:43.113 --> 00:00:48.713
the paint is applied, so the picture grows the way a person would paint. Jake Eaton, who works

00:00:48.713 --> 00:00:54.833
at Anthropic, counts roughly 700,000 strokes in one parking garage scene that looks like a real

00:00:54.833 --> 00:01:00.193
photo. The finished picture still feels a bit handmade, even when the scene looks realistic.

00:01:00.953 --> 00:01:05.833
Originality gets harder to define, because the system repeats the same stroke-by-stroke actions

00:01:05.833 --> 00:01:11.653
a painter would take. If you draw, paint, or design for fun, this follows the steps you already

00:01:11.653 --> 00:01:17.593
use, rather than treating a picture as pure math. For beginners, it is a window onto AI that

00:01:17.593 --> 00:01:23.513
supports hands-on creative work instead of replacing it. Teens chasing art hobbies or design careers

00:01:23.513 --> 00:01:29.193
can treat it as a partner that already speaks their process. Open Clawed Fable 5.2 on

00:01:29.193 --> 00:01:34.433
Anthropics platforms, type a description of a place you know well, and notice how texture

00:01:34.433 --> 00:01:40.225
appears as the strokes pile up. Art students can set that output beside a usual all-at-once

00:01:40.225 --> 00:01:45.565
picture, and notice the difference in texture and feel. Stroke order does the real work, and

00:01:45.565 --> 00:01:51.385
big shapes land first, long before any gleam or wood grain exists. Art class already

00:01:51.385 --> 00:01:56.545
uses this order, blocking in the building, then the shadows, then the gleam on a window.

00:01:57.065 --> 00:01:59.665
The next thousands of strokes add shadows and highlights.

00:02:00.225 --> 00:02:05.125
The final ones put in tiny details, like a reflection on a car or the grain of wood.

00:02:05.605 --> 00:02:10.365
Those layers interact naturally, so later paint sits on earlier paint instead of ignoring

00:02:10.365 --> 00:02:10.665
it.

00:02:11.165 --> 00:02:15.705
A finished garage is thousands of small, ordered choices stacked in the sequence a student

00:02:15.705 --> 00:02:16.885
painter already knows.

00:02:16.885 --> 00:02:22.085
Once you can name those three stages, the picture stops being a mystery box and starts

00:02:22.085 --> 00:02:24.545
being a process you could explain to someone else.

00:02:25.611 --> 00:02:30.311
Australia's Central Bank, which watches that country's economy, noticed a hiring shift

00:02:30.311 --> 00:02:32.011
after chat GPT arrived.

00:02:32.531 --> 00:02:37.331
Chat GPT is a chatbot people type questions into, and tools like it are what the bank

00:02:37.331 --> 00:02:37.931
is tracking.

00:02:38.691 --> 00:02:44.191
Hiring and job ads for people aged 15 to 24 in software development and customer service

00:02:44.191 --> 00:02:46.671
felt harder than they did for older workers.

00:02:47.191 --> 00:02:49.671
The change shows up first in the entry-level work

00:02:49.671 --> 00:02:51.731
many students and recent graduates do.

00:02:52.371 --> 00:02:55.031
Beginner jobs in those fields may get harder to find

00:02:55.431 --> 00:02:58.511
while work that uses AI alongside people could grow.

00:02:59.271 --> 00:03:00.611
Researchers are careful to say

00:03:00.611 --> 00:03:02.651
the wider economy also plays a role.

00:03:03.211 --> 00:03:04.751
So this is not the full picture yet.

00:03:05.451 --> 00:03:07.611
Mike Major says do not leap to a verdict

00:03:07.611 --> 00:03:10.131
because a shifting economy and remote work

00:03:10.131 --> 00:03:11.591
could also produce those numbers.

00:03:11.591 --> 00:03:18.191
Open a career site you already use, type entry-level jobs AI into the search box, and notice

